   "text": {
    "L1": "Leaves exchange gases through tiny pores called stomata.",
    "L2": "A common myth says that no gases pass through stomata at night. That is not true.",
    "L3": "Each stoma is controlled by two guard cells, which open and close it.",
    "L4": "In sunlight, photosynthesis is faster than respiration, so carbon dioxide enters overall and oxygen leaves overall.",
    "L5": "In darkness, photosynthesis stops but respiration continues. Oxygen enters and carbon dioxide leaves.",
    "L6": "Water vapour also escapes through stomata. This loss is called transpiration and can cool the plant.",
    "L7": "Now check the gas that enters a leaf at midnight."
   },
